GEMM-RS: overlap global loads with MFMAs in the mainloop, 269.96 -> 256.09 us

E1(b). The k-loop previously issued the next block's global->LDS copy as a
SYNCHRONOUS operation: global_load_dwordx4, then s_waitcnt vmcnt(0), then
ds_write, then s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0) -- twice, because the 32 KB BK=32 slab was
drained in two serialized halves -- all before the 64 MFMAs could issue. The
LDS double buffering was real in the allocation and in the dependency
structure, but it bought no latency overlap at all, and with LDS at the 64 KB
ceiling there is only 1 CTA/CU so no second wave could cover the stall either.

Split kittens::load into an issue half and a commit half (copied into our own
adapter; include/** is untouched), then hoisted the issue above the MFMA block
and sank the wait and the LDS commit below it. The ISA confirms it held:
s_waitcnt vmcnt(0) now sits after all 64 MFMAs and all four global_load_dwordx4
are in flight together. Two exposed round trips per k-iteration became one
covered one.

Landed in two arms, and the order mattered:

- P3, splitting the k-step into two BK/2 halves, is timing-neutral (+0.06%) but
  clears the 12 B scratch and both VGPR spills and frees 23 registers on the
  256-row configs. Bit-exact, since mma_ABt already chains k ascending per
  accumulator element. It is what pays for E1(b), which lands at 246/248 VGPR
  against the 256 cap.
- E1(b) then takes the geomean 269.96 -> 256.09 us (-5.14%), reproduced at
  255.59 us on an independent repeat. Per shape -10.0 / -7.8 / -5.4 / -0.7 /
  -2.4 / -4.3 percent.

Both arms passed the full ladder: 17/17 shapes at both 1e-2 and 2e-3, all three
negative controls failing as designed, 600-epoch soak with epoch and signal
cells exact.

The near-miss is worth reading, because it would have shipped silently. A bare
s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0) does NOT protect a register loaded from LDS: it has no
operands, so it creates no data dependence, the ds_read is an asm volatile
whose output the compiler believes is live immediately, and v_mfma is a plain
intrinsic with no memory effects. The scheduler hoisted MFMAs above the wait in
exactly the three K_TAIL=false instantiations, failing 14 of 17 shapes with
max|diff| between 1e19 and 9e30 and no error bit set. The K_TAIL=true ones were
clean only because mask_a_k_tail happened to fragment the block. Fixed by
laundering each base tile through asm volatile("" : "+v"(...)) after the wait,
at zero instruction and register cost. The rule: when LDS traffic is hidden
behind asm volatile, the wait and its consumer must be tied by a data
dependence, not by program order.

lds_race_check.sh catches this class mechanically for both counters in about 30
seconds; run it after any mainloop edit. It also confirms the separately
recorded latent last-k-iteration race is now repaired.

Also fixes a self-inflicted gate defect: gate_ladder.sh asserted 7 M2
instantiation rows, but exp_04 retiled config row 1 onto the pre-existing
<32,64,64,false> template, leaving 6 distinct instantiations (rows 4 and 5 also
share <256,256,32,false>). The committed ladder could not pass on the committed
baseline. Now a named M2_EXPECT constant, counting distinct instantiations
rather than config rows.
